1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:04,240
let's move now to process scheduling we
have a dedicated lecture about

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:10,480
scheduling so this is just a
simple introduction here so

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,880
we said that the context switch needs to
be fast

4
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,800
this implies that when we need to decide
what is the next

5
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,880
process that should run we should be
efficient

6
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:26,880
now how efficient the operating system
is

7
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:30,000
depends on the data structures that it
uses

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,600
to maintain the information about the
processes or the process control blocks

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,280
here you have a first example of the
tradeoffs I mentioned between space and

10
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:42,840
time complexity
you can keep everything in a single data

11
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:47,520
structure
that is potentially simple it reduces

12
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:53,039
space complexity but then it's not
optimal for time complexity if you keep

13
00:00:53,039 --> 00:00:56,960
everything inside
the same data structure you might need

14
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,079
to traverse
the entire data structure to find out

15
00:01:00,079 --> 00:01:03,440
which is the next program that you want

16
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:06,960
to run
so the operating system does something

17
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:13,200
smarter than this
it actually keeps different queues

18
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,240
in which it stores the process control
blocks

19
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,600
one queue is the queue of processes that
are ready

20
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:23,840
or process control 
blocks that refer to processes that are

21
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:28,159
ready and then there are different
queues depending on the input output

22
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:32,320
device
okay so you can have a queue that is

23
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:35,600
containing only the process control
blocks

24
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,520
of processes that are waiting for the
disk

25
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:46,399
and to say that one process
changes its state from waiting to ready

26
00:01:46,399 --> 00:01:50,240
could actually mean that the process
control block of that process

27
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:56,640
is removed from the queue that refers to
a disk unit and it's added back to the

28
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:03,040
queue of the ready processes
this organization helps in reducing the

29
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,799
context switch time
because if you want to know what is the

30
00:02:06,799 --> 00:02:10,640
next ready process to run
you don't need to check among all the

31
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,080
process control blocks you have
but you can just pick the next in the

32
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:19,840
queue of the process control blocks that
are about ready processes

33
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:24,480
so this is the let's say

34
00:02:24,959 --> 00:02:30,080
life cycle of the process once it starts
it gets into the ready queue

35
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:33,599
and it gets some CPU time at a certain
point

36
00:02:33,599 --> 00:02:39,680
it might just terminate or
some other things could happen there can

37
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:44,080
be a request 
and then it will go in the queue of

38
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,560
some I/O device until

39
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:55,599
the I/O is done
it might be that it's the time slice

40
00:02:55,599 --> 00:02:58,640
that is given expires the operating
system

41
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:03,040
might say each program runs
for 10 milliseconds there is always this

42
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:07,680
clock ticking and creating interrupts
let's say every second so sometimes the

43
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,480
operating system might wake up
and check how much time it does

44
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:18,400
let a certain process run and then put
it back to the ready queue we will see

45
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,920
that processes can
fork a child or better said

46
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:25,200
they can create other processes
and this could be a reason why you stop

47
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,599
running this process and you start
running

48
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:32,959
a different one or they could be
just waiting for an interrupt

49
00:03:32,959 --> 00:03:36,640
in which they will stay in this
waiting state until they get

50
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:41,360
an interrupt and then they will go back
to the ready queue

51
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:45,440
now you have both short term schedulers
and long term

52
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:50,720
schedulers as the name suggests the
short time

53
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:53,840
scheduler selects the process that
should be executed next

54
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:59,519
so the process that should get the CPU
and this is maybe the only

55
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:02,480
scheduler you ever for sure the
scheduler you need because it's the one

56
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:05,519
taking decision immediately about what
to run next

57
00:04:05,519 --> 00:04:11,120
you can also have long-term
schedulers so it might be that

58
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:15,040
there are
processes that need to be put to sleep

59
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,560
because there are not enough resources
we would see this thing in a

60
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:22,400
certain number of lectures when we talk
about virtual memory

61
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:26,880
okay so these are let's say taking
decision on a larger

62
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:32,560
time frame than the short term schedules

